em would produce sufficiently sharp near-return blocks for which the left side of \\eqref{eq:aperiodic-linear-form} is too small relative to known lower bounds for linear forms in logarithms, or would use many moderate block relations through $S$-unit/subspace methods.","+\\bibitem{KurtzSimon} S.~A.~Kurtz, J.~Simon, The undecidability of the","+generalized Collatz problem, in \\emph{TAMC 2007}, LNCS 4484,","+Springer, 2007, 542--553."," ","-This is not presently standard linear-forms technology.  It is a hybrid of:","-\\[","-  \\text{linear forms in logarithms}","-  +","-  \\text{ergodic optimization/calibrated measures}","-  +","-  \\text{symbolic complexity}","-  +","-  \\text{mixed-adic realizability}.","-\\]","+\\bibitem{DowneyHirschfeldt} R.~Downey, D.~Hirschfeldt,","+\\emph{Algorithmic Randomness and Complexity}, Springer, 2010."," ","-\\section{Interpretation}","+\\bibitem{Jenkinson} O.~Jenkinson, Ergodic optimization in dynamical","+systems, \\emph{Ergodic Theory Dynam. Systems} 39 (2019), 2593--2618."," ","-The physical/spectral picture is now clearer.  The Collatz system has not been placed into a self-adjoint Hamiltonian or Selberg-trace framework.  The natural finite object is instead non-self-adjoint: a twisted transfer operator with arithmetic phases, killed/strip dynamics, and max-plus descent certificates.  The self-adjoint objects $T^*T$ are norm-control auxiliaries, not fundamental Hamiltonians.","+\\bibitem{Butkovic} P.~Butkovi\\v{c}, \\emph{Max-linear Systems: Theory","+and Algorithms}, Springer, 2010."," ","-The final obstruction is therefore not a missing eigenvalue computation.  It is a deterministic rigidity problem: show that no single positive integer orbit can realize the high-complexity, mixed-adic, non-coboundary-biased traps that remain after all annealed and low-complexity mechanisms have been eliminated.","+\\bibitem{Tao} T.~Tao, Almost all orbits of the Collatz map attain","+almost bounded values, \\emph{Forum Math. Pi} 10 (2022), e12."," ","-\\section{Current endpoint}","+\\bibitem{KrasikovLagarias} I.~Krasikov, J.~C.~Lagarias, Bounds for","+the $3x+1$ problem using difference inequalities, \\emph{Acta Arith.}","+109 (2003), 237--258."," ","-The program has reached the following precise endpoint.","+\\bibitem{SimonsDeWeger} J.~L.~Simons, B.~M.~M.~de~Weger, Theoretical","+and computational bounds for $m$-cycles of the $3n+1$ problem,","+\\emph{Acta Arith.} 117 (2005), 51--70."," ","-\\begin{center}","-\\fbox{\\begin{minipage}{0.92\\textwidth}","-Any counterexample must be a positive-realizable valuation sequence that is mean-critical, non-coboundary shadow-biased, high-complexity inside every long banded window, coherent in the mixed $2^a3^b$ tower, and resistant to every finite max-plus descent certificate.  Proving Collatz in this framework requires a new deterministic rigidity theorem excluding such objects, most plausibly by combining banded $S$-unit/subspace rigidity with mixed-tower max-plus certificates.","-\\end{minipage}}","-\\end{center}","+\\bibitem{Hercher} C.~Hercher, There are no Collatz $m$-cycles with","+$m\\le91$, \\emph{J. Integer Seq.} 26 (2023), Art. 23.3.5."," ","-This is the remaining obstruction in its sharpest current form.","+\\bibitem{Barina} D.~Barina, Convergence verification of the Collatz","+problem, \\emph{J. Supercomputing} 77 (2021), 2681--2688."," ","-\\begin{thebibliography}{9}","+\\bibitem{Terras} R.~Terras, A stopping time problem on the positive","+integers, \\emph{Acta Arith.} 30 (1976), 241--252."," ","-\\bibitem{Baker}","-A. Baker, \\emph{Linear forms in the logarithms of algebraic numbers}, Mathematika 13 (1966), 204--216.","+\\bibitem{LagariasSurvey} J.~C.~Lagarias, The $3x+1$ problem and its","+generalizations, \\emph{Amer. Math. Monthly} 92 (1985), 3--23."," ","-\\bibitem{Bugeaud}","-Y. Bugeaud, \\emph{Linear Forms in Logarithms and Applications}, IRMA Lectures in Mathematics and Theoretical Physics, European Mathematical Society, 2018.","+\\bibitem{